  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts and it passes typecheck. I could not run it in a browser, so the animation, the camera and the shadows are untested.\n\n- **Bird:** a body, a head with a cone beak and two eyes, a five-feather tail fan, and two wings. Each wing has an inner and an outer segment joined at a hinge.\n- **Flight:** the bird circles at a 6-unit radius at constant height, with an 8-second period. It faces its direction of travel and banks into the turn.\n- **Wings and body:** the flap is 2 Hz, so there are exactly 16 flaps per lap and the loop has no jump. The outer segments lag the inner ones by about 0.9 rad, and the body rises and falls by 0.1 units each flap.\n- **Scene:** a ground disc, cone trees and a few rocks. The sky-colored background has a hemisphere light plus one shadow-casting directional light.\n- **Camera:** it sits 7.5 units outside the circle, behind the bird along its path, and looks at the bird each frame. I move it with `app.camera?.setPose`, which the docs say to avoid for the orbit camera. If that call does nothing, the camera stays fixed at its starting position and the bird will leave the view.\n- **Ready flag:** `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set on the first animation frame after `app.ready()`.\n\nI also assumed standard right-handed rotation signs for the banking and the wing flaps. If the bird banks away from the turn or the wings flap the wrong way, those signs need flipping.\n\n",
